abs_parts = [
 f"Background: Authors, funders and evaluators use a journal's reported or past time to acceptance as a forecast of how long a new submission will take, but such statistics have not been tested out of time, and claims that a journal accepts papers 'within X days' are rarely checked.",
 f"Methods: We used publisher-deposited received and accepted dates for {M['n_records']:,} research articles in {M['n_journals']} Springer Nature, Wiley and Taylor & Francis journals ({M['n_journals_forecast']} evaluable). At forecast origins in January 2023 and January 2024, and in a rolling quarterly panel, forecasts built only from papers already visible were scored against the {M['n_target_papers']:,} papers submitted in the following six months, using pinball loss, interval coverage and event-probability scores. Inference used journal-cluster and imprint-cluster bootstraps. The received date was checked against preprint posting dates.",
 f"Results: Journal identity explained {pc(vc['sigma2_journal']/tot)} (95% CI {pc(vci['icc_journal'][0])}–{pc(vci['icc_journal'][1])}) of the variance of log acceptance time. Journal-specific forecasts lowered loss by {pc(fm['F0']['skill'])} relative to publisher-pooled forecasts, but 80% intervals spanned about five-fold in days and covered {pc(0.8+fm['F0']['cov80_minus'])} of later papers (journal average; {pc(C['paper_level_coverage_F0'])} of papers; imprint-cluster CI {pt(sc['lo'])} to {pt(sc['hi'])} points around nominal). The shortfall was absent under random splitting ({pt(Y2['cov_exchangeable']['est'])} points) and in a stationary simulation, indicating non-exchangeability between cohorts. Lognormal, shrinkage and learned forecasters did not beat empirical quantiles of the last 12 months. A quantile-level recalibration learned on earlier cohorts restored coverage ({pt(CM['F9_cov80_minus_nominal']['mean'])} points) without loss in accuracy. {pc(g[(72,0.5)]['share'])} of journal-origins accepted at least half of their papers within 72 days ({pc(min(v['share_P72_ge_0_5'] for k, v in Y3.items() if isinstance(v, dict) and 'shortfall' in v))}–{pc(max(v['share_P72_ge_0_5'] for k, v in Y3.items() if isinstance(v, dict) and 'shortfall' in v))} across robustness variants) and {pc(g[(72,0.8)]['share'])} at least 80%. In Springer Nature journals with Research Square preprints, {pc(X1sn['share_received_le_posted_plus_14'],0)} of received dates were no later than 14 days after the preprint was posted, supporting the received date as the original submission date; this could not be tested for Wiley and Taylor & Francis.",
 "Conclusions: A journal's last year of acceptance times is a useful but wide and slightly over-confident forecast. Reports of editorial-process statistics should give quantiles, cohort definitions and uncertainty, and 'within X days' claims should not be read as guarantees. Findings are conditional on acceptance, on three publishers and on publisher-defined dates."]
