# ---------------------------------------------------------------- background
H("Background")
P("Journals and publishers advertise speed, and authors, funders and evaluators repeat such statements when choosing where to publish or when setting expectations about time to acceptance. Whether the statistics behind these statements—usually a median or average of past papers—are a usable forecast of the experience of the next paper has not, to our knowledge, been tested. The question is a matter of reporting integrity in editorial processes: statistics that are defined inconsistently or presented without uncertainty can mislead the people who rely on them ⟦phillips2025,bilalli2021,andersen2021⟧.")
P("Empirical work has described how long peer review and publication take. In a stratified sample of 2,700 papers from 135 journals, most of the variance in submission-to-acceptance time lay among articles within a journal ⟦bjork2013⟧. Studies have documented trends and determinants of delay in economics, accounting, medicine and across PubMed ⟦ellison2002,argiles2023,sebo2019,luwel2020,hadavand2024,himmelstein⟧, a COVID-19 speed-up ⟦horbach2020⟧, a strain on publishing capacity ⟦hanson2024⟧, and the fact that the definition and reporting of review times differ across journals and from article-level values ⟦phillips2025,bilalli2021,andersen2021⟧. Stage-wise models of one editorial workflow exist ⟦mrowinski2016⟧. A registered-style plan for a PubMed-wide analysis intends to predict article-level acceptance lag with a random training/test split ⟦dianovics_lifecycle⟧; it states that it was not preregistered, and no results were available when we searched. Commercial tools already present historical medians as planning aids; we found no validation of them.")
P("What we did not find is an evaluation of journal-level turnaround statistics as forecasts: scored against later outcomes, using only information visible when the forecast is made, and reporting calibration. We ask: (RQ1) how much of the variance of acceptance time lies between journals, between years and between articles; (RQ2) how accurate and calibrated are forecasts built from a journal's visible past, and which simple estimator is best; (RQ3) why do such forecasts miscalibrate; (RQ4) how often is a claim that a journal accepts a given share of papers within 72 days supported, and are such claims reliable out of time; (RQ5) do features available around submission add information; and (RQ6) is the publisher-deposited received date a valid measure of original submission? RQ1 extends earlier work to a larger and more recent dataset; RQ2–RQ6 are, as far as a non-systematic search could establish, new. The study is empirical meta-research and makes no causal claims.")
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- methods
H("Methods")
H("Design, pre-specification and order of events", 2)
P("The first-cycle protocol (frame, cleaning rules, cohorts, forecasters, metrics, seven contrasts and five hypotheses) was committed to the project's git history before any forecast error was computed; this is a pre-specification by the analyst, not a registered report, and no external time stamp exists. In a second cycle, an independent review identified weaknesses and further experiments were added. Their order is disclosed: the received-date probe, a recalibration experiment and a learned-forecaster experiment were designed in code and run once before their protocol text was written; the dependence, data-quality and calibrated-probability experiments and the follow-up panel, null/simulation, robustness and article-level experiments were specified and committed before being run. All amendments, post-hoc analyses and failed or mis-specified thresholds are logged in the repository (deviations D1–D11 and cycle-2 log). Seven of the first-cycle hypotheses were evaluated as pre-specified; results that did not support them are reported.")
H("Data", 2)
P(f"Article-level received and accepted dates are deposited in Crossref by Springer Nature, Wiley and Taylor & Francis. For each publisher we took the 1,000 ISSNs with most journal articles published in 2023, merged print and online ISSNs ({M['frame_raw_journals']:,} journals), and kept journals in which at least 80% of 40 random 2023 records carried both dates and the estimated number of dated 2023 articles was 150–3,000 ({M['frame_journals']} entries; two ISSN pairs were not merged, leaving {M['n_journals']} distinct journals with data). All records published from 1 January 2021 to 20 October 2026 were downloaded. The estimand is D, the number of days from received to accepted, conditional on acceptance. Records were cleaned with rules fixed in advance: duplicate DOIs, D<1 or >1,460 days, acceptance more than seven days after first online publication, and non-research items by title or fewer than five references. Of {M['n_raw_dated']:,} dated records, {M['n_records']:,} remained; median D was {M['D_quantiles']['0.5']:.0f} days. Journals were assigned to an imprint by the modal DOI prefix of their articles (Springer, BioMed Central, Nature, Wiley, Blackwell, Taylor & Francis, other).")
H("Validity of the received date", 2)
P(f"Published articles that Crossref links to a preprint were sampled (up to 30 per journal; {X1['n_records']:,} articles in {X1['n_journals']} journals; the collection was interrupted and uses the part written). Research Square 'In Review' preprints are posted when a manuscript enters review at a Springer Nature journal; if the received date is the original submission date, received minus posting date should be near zero or slightly negative, and rarely large and positive. We report the share with received no later than 14 days after posting and the share more than 90 days after posting. Wiley and Taylor & Francis articles with Research Square preprints are independent postings and cannot validate their dates.")
H("Forecasting task", 2)
P("Origins were 1 January 2023 and 1 January 2024 (Figure 1). At an origin the author's information set contains papers that were first online before the origin and already received and accepted. The target cohort comprises all papers received in the following 182 days. A journal-origin was eligible with at least 100 visible papers in the preceding 12 months and 50 target papers. Forecasters of the distribution of ln D were evaluated at 19 quantile levels: F0, empirical quantiles of the last 12 months (Hazen definition ⟦hyndman1996⟧); F1, the same over 24 months; F2, publisher-pooled quantiles ignoring journal identity; F3, a lognormal fitted by maximum likelihood; F4, an empirical-Bayes lognormal ⟦dersimonian1986,efron1973⟧; F6, exponentially time-decayed empirical quantiles (half-life 182.5 days). Two cycle-2 forecasters used earlier cohorts only: R, F0 with quantile levels recalibrated by the empirical distribution of probability integral transform values of earlier target cohorts (fitted on 2022 targets for the 2024 origin and on 2022–2023 targets for 2025), and L, a gradient-boosted quantile regression ⟦ke2017⟧ on journal-level features (past quantiles, size, trend, volume growth, publisher).")
doc.add_picture(f"{ROOT}/figures/fig1_protocol_schematic.png", width=Inches(6.0)); doc.paragraphs[-1].alignment = WD_ALIGN_PARAGRAPH.CENTER
P("Figure 1. Conceptual schematic (not data) of the out-of-time protocol. Forecasts use only the visible training set and are scored against papers submitted after the origin.", size=9.5, space=10)
H("Scoring and inference", 2)
P("The primary score is the mean pinball loss over quantile levels on the ln-day scale ⟦koenker1978,gneiting2007⟧; secondary scores are coverage of central intervals, the interval score ⟦winkler1972⟧, Brier scores for the events D ≤ 72, 120 and 180 days, and probability-integral-transform histograms ⟦gneiting2007b⟧. Journals are the unit of analysis. Because journals of one imprint are correlated and the two origins overlap, uncertainty is reported with journal-cluster bootstraps ⟦efron1979,field2007⟧ and, for the main results, with imprint-cluster bootstraps, across-imprint tests, stratified bootstraps, leave-one-publisher-out estimates and, for the rolling panel, a two-way (imprint × origin) bootstrap. Paired comparisons use Wilcoxon signed-rank tests ⟦wilcoxon1945⟧ with Holm correction; shares use Clopper–Pearson intervals ⟦clopper1934⟧. Variance components of ln D (journal, journal × year, residual) were estimated by method of moments from acceptance years 2021–2025.")
H("Additional analyses", 2)
P("A rolling panel used quarterly origins from January 2022 to April 2024, 91-day target cohorts and a common follow-up cap of 480 days. Two null worlds tested the cause of any under-coverage: an exchangeable benchmark (random split within a journal's complete submission cohort at the observed sample sizes) and a generative simulation without drift in which arrivals grew at each journal's observed rate, durations were drawn from the journal's own target distribution, and the visibility rule was applied. Robustness variants excluded records with fewer than 10 or 15 references, records accepted on days with batch acceptances, journals with at least 10% of papers accepted within 14 days, and 75 journals flagged for fast-lane or implausibly short durations. A strictly out-of-time article-level model was trained on 2022 targets and tested on 2024 targets with author count, received month and weekday (with or without reference count), against a control with the journal quantile only.")
H("Use of AI", 2)
P("The analysis code and drafts of this manuscript were produced with an AI system (Claude, Anthropic) acting as an autonomous research assistant in a sandboxed environment; separately launched instances of the same model family performed adversarial reviews, a prior-art search and a reproducibility audit. No human expert has reviewed the work. The authors must verify the analyses and take responsibility for the content.")
